**user**:
> What does it mean when I see a value like "-3.40282e+38" in a D-Infinity TIFF generated by TauDEM?
**assistant**:
> In TauDEM D-Infinity grids, a value like `-3.40282e+38` is essentially a **NoData flag** (i.e., “this cell has no valid value”).
> 
> More specifically:
> 
> - `3.40282e+38` is approximately the **maximum finite value for a 32-bit IEEE floating-point number** (`FLT_MAX`).
> - TauDEM (and many other tools) use **-FLT_MAX** (≈ `-3.4028235e+38`) to mark:
>   - Cells outside the watershed or outside the DEM extent
>   - Masked-out areas
>   - Places where a flow direction or other D-Infinity quantity cannot be computed
> 
> So if you see `-3.40282e+38` in a D-Infinity TIFF:
> 
> - Treat it as **missing / NoData**, not a real physical or geometric value.
> - In GIS software, you can set that value as the raster’s NoData value so it’s ignored in analyses and display.
